  <h2>Why people mix them up</h2>
  <p>Cambodia and Thailand share a long border and a long history. Both cuisines grew up around rice, fresh herbs, fish sauce and grilled meats. So it's no surprise that a first bite of Cambodian food can remind people of Thai food.</p>
  <p>But in the US, Thai restaurants are everywhere and Cambodian restaurants are rare. Many people simply haven't had a chance to taste the difference. This guide is a starting point, not the final word. Every family cooks a little differently in both countries.</p>

  <h2>Where they overlap</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rice at the center.</strong> In both kitchens, rice is the base of the meal.</li>
    <li><strong>The same herb family.</strong> Lemongrass, galangal, makrut lime leaves, garlic and shallots show up in both.</li>
    <li><strong>Fish sauce.</strong> Both use it for salty, savory depth.</li>
    <li><strong>Grilled street food.</strong> Skewers and grilled meats are loved on both sides of the border.</li>
    <li><strong>Fresh and pickled sides.</strong> Crunchy vegetables balance rich dishes in both cuisines.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where they differ</h2>
  <h3>The flavor base</h3>
  <p>Many Thai dishes start with a curry paste built around chilies. Many Cambodian dishes start with <a href="/guides/what-is-kreung">kreung</a>, a paste built around fresh herbs like lemongrass and turmeric. Kreung is fragrant first and spicy second, if at all.</p>
  <h3>Heat</h3>
  <p>Thai food is known for its heat. Cambodian food is usually milder in the pot, with chilies and sauces on the side so each person can add their own. If you're cooking for a group with mixed spice tolerance, that's a real plus.</p>
  <h3>Sweetness</h3>
  <p>Thai cooking often balances sweet, sour, salty and spicy, with palm sugar playing a big role. Cambodian cooking tends to lean more on sour and savory notes than on sweetness.</p>
  <h3>Fermented flavors</h3>
  <p>Prahok, a fermented fish paste, is a signature of Cambodian cooking. It adds a deep, funky, salty flavor to dips, soups and stir-fries. It's one of the clearest markers of Cambodian food.</p>
  <h3>Signature dishes</h3>
  <p>Thai food is known around the world for dishes like pad thai and green curry. Cambodian classics include amok (a fragrant steamed curry), samlor (soups and stews) and grilled meats marinated in kreung.</p>

